const POSE_DETAILS = {
  'Front Double Biceps': {icon:'frontDoubleBiceps', video:'front double biceps bodybuilding pose tutorial', points:[
    'Setup: feet shoulder-width, one foot slightly forward and turned out slightly to add a visual taper down to the ankle — never stand perfectly square, it flattens your physique.',
    'Execution: raise both arms to just above shoulder height, rotate your wrists inward as you flex to peak the biceps at their highest point, not just tense them.',
    'Whole-body integration: this isn\'t an arm pose — flex your abs and quads hard simultaneously, and spread your lats slightly to widen your entire upper body silhouette, not just your arms.',
    'Common mistake: shrugging the shoulders up toward the ears. This shortens your neck and swallows your delts — keep the traps down and the neck long so the shoulder-to-neck line stays sharp.',
    'Judging insight: judges scan symmetry left-to-right first. Practice in a mirror until both sides peak at the exact same height and thickness — a lopsided flex costs more than a slightly smaller peak.',
  ]},
  'Front Lat Spread': {icon:'frontDoubleBiceps', video:'front lat spread bodybuilding pose tutorial', points:[
    'Setup: grip your waist or lower lats with fingers pointing forward, elbows driving forward and slightly down.',
    'Execution: flare your lats outward by driving your elbows toward each other in front of your body — the spread comes from the lats pulling wide, not from the arms alone.',
    'Whole-body integration: keep your chest lifted and abs braced hard — this pose lives or dies on your V-taper, so a collapsed chest kills the illusion of width no matter how wide your lats spread.',
    'Common mistake: rounding the shoulders forward to try to force more spread. That reads as a slouch on stage, not width — the width has to come from underneath the arms, not from posture collapse.',
    'Judging insight: the widest visual point should be your lats, not your elbows. Practice finding the exact elbow angle in a mirror that maximizes lat width without your elbows becoming the widest part of the pose.',
  ]},
  'Side Chest': {icon:'sideChest', video:'side chest bodybuilding pose tutorial', points:[
    'Setup: turn to your strongest side (usually determined by which bicep peaks higher or which pec has better shape), grip your near wrist with your far hand.',
    'Execution: drive your chest up and out by pressing your gripped arms downward, which forces the chest to expand and separate from the shoulder.',
    'Whole-body integration: this is a full-body pose — flex your near-side bicep, quad, and calf all at the same time for a fully "locked-in" look; a flexed chest with a soft leg reads as an unfinished pose.',
    'Common mistake: turning the rear foot straight rather than out toward the judges — a flat rear foot hides your calf entirely, which is a wasted opportunity in a pose built around leg conditioning too.',
    'Judging insight: the pec-to-shoulder separation is what judges are actually scoring here, not raw chest size. A well-defined line between chest and delt beats a bigger but blended-looking chest every time.',
  ]},
  'Back Double Biceps': {icon:'backDoubleBiceps', video:'back double biceps bodybuilding pose tutorial', points:[
    'Setup: same arm position as front double biceps, but your back is now on display — stand with feet slightly narrower to keep the lower body from distracting from the back width.',
    'Execution: flare your lats hard while arching your lower back very slightly to create thickness through the middle back, not just width at the top.',
    'Whole-body integration: come up slightly onto one foot to flex that calf and show separation — alternating which foot you rise on between practice reps helps you find your better calf.',
    'Common mistake: dropping the head to look down at the floor. This kills the trap-to-back line completely — keep your chin level or very slightly up so the traps show their full development.',
    'Judging insight: back width vs. back thickness are graded somewhat separately in judges\' minds — if your width is your strength, hold the flare a beat longer before adding the arch; if thickness is your strength, add the arch earlier.',
  ]},
  'Back Lat Spread': {icon:'backDoubleBiceps', video:'back lat spread bodybuilding pose tutorial', points:[
    'Setup: grip your waist from behind, elbows driving out and slightly back rather than just out to the sides.',
    'Execution: spread your lats as wide as physically possible while actively keeping your lower back tight and braced, not just relaxed.',
    'Whole-body integration: flex your glutes and hamstrings along with your back — a back double lat spread with soft legs looks incomplete next to a competitor who\'s flexed head to toe.',
    'Common mistake: pulling the elbows purely out to the sides instead of back. Elbows-back recruits more lat fiber and creates a deeper V than elbows-out, which mostly just moves the arms.',
    'Judging insight: this is one of the few poses where a smaller person with better lat insertions can visually out-width a bigger competitor — work the mind-muscle connection on lat spread specifically, not just general back training.',
  ]},
  'Side Triceps': {icon:'sideTriceps', video:'side triceps bodybuilding pose tutorial', points:[
    'Setup: turn to your side, extend your near arm down and back, gripping your wrist with your far hand behind your back.',
    'Execution: flex the triceps hard through full extension — a bent elbow here looks soft; full lockout is what actually shows the horseshoe shape.',
    'Whole-body integration: pull your near shoulder back so you don\'t look hunched, and flex through your whole rear leg (calf and hamstring), not just the arm — this is a full side-profile pose, not just a triceps shot.',
    'Common mistake: letting the chest cave forward while focusing on the arm. A collapsed chest undermines the whole silhouette even if the triceps flex is perfect.',
    'Judging insight: the horseshoe (the three heads of the triceps separating) is what separates a good side triceps from a great one — this responds specifically to close-grip pressing and overhead extension work, not just general arm volume.',
  ]},
  'Abdominal & Thigh': {icon:'abdominalThigh', video:'abdominal and thigh bodybuilding pose tutorial', points:[
    'Setup: hands laced behind your head, one leg extended forward on the ball of the foot, weight mostly on your standing leg.',
    'Execution: crunch your torso forward and down sharply while flexing your extended leg\'s quad hard — the crunch and the quad flex should hit at the exact same instant.',
    'Whole-body integration: exhale sharply and completely as you hit the pose — a held breath keeps your abdominal wall pushed out, which flattens the very separation you\'re trying to show.',
    'Common mistake: locking the extended knee out hard. A slightly soft knee actually shows more of the quad sweep (the teardrop muscle) than a fully locked, tensed leg does.',
    'Judging insight: this pose rewards low body fat more than almost any other — the muscle detail matters, but stage conditioning on the day is what actually makes or breaks this specific pose versus others where muscle shape can compensate.',
  ]},
  'Most Muscular': {icon:'mostMuscular', video:'most muscular bodybuilding pose tutorial', points:[
    'Setup: choose your variation ahead of time and drill it specifically — crab (arms down and in, knuckles down) or hands-clasped (fingers interlocked at waist height) are both accepted, but each recruits slightly different muscles.',
    'Execution: crunch your entire upper body inward at once — traps up, chest driving down and in, arms pressing toward center — this is a whole-torso contraction, not an arm flex.',
    'Whole-body integration: drive your shoulders forward and slightly down while keeping your chin tucked — this maximizes the trap-to-chest crunch that defines the pose.',
    'Common mistake: only flexing the arms and forgetting the chest and traps. A most muscular that\'s all forearms and biceps misses the point of the pose entirely.',
    'Judging insight: this is the pose where raw density and hardness show most clearly — it\'s often used as a tiebreaker between similarly-sized competitors, so it rewards whoever has done more peak-week conditioning work, not just more mass.',
  ]},
  'Quarter Turn': {icon:'quarterTurn', video:'bodybuilding quarter turns posing tutorial', points:[
    'Setup: stand tall and relaxed, weight slightly on your back foot, front foot turned out about 30-45°.',
    'Execution: shoulders back and down, chest lifted, core gently engaged — this is presentation and poise, not maximal muscular flexing.',
    'Whole-body integration: keep the same posture and energy through all four angles (front, side, back, side) — a competitor who "checks out" between turns loses points versus one who holds presence the entire time.',
    'Common mistake: over-flexing in an attempt to look more athletic. Judges in these divisions are specifically scoring a relaxed, confident look — visible straining reads as trying too hard, not as fitness.',
    'Judging insight: practice the turn itself, not just the poses — a crisp, confident quarter turn on the judges\' call is part of what\'s being scored, independent of how good your physique looks standing still.',
  ]},
  'Side Profile Pose': {icon:'quarterTurn', video:'bikini side profile pose tutorial', points:[
    'Setup: turn fully to your best side, weight on your back leg, front leg extended slightly forward with a soft knee.',
    'Execution: lift your ribcage and lengthen through your whole side body — think tall and long, not flexed or braced.',
    'Whole-body integration: keep your glutes and hamstrings gently engaged (not flexed hard) to maintain a clean line from shoulder to heel.',
    'Common mistake: collapsing at the waist or letting the hip drop — this breaks the long, clean silhouette that this pose is built to show off.',
    'Judging insight: this pose is about the waist-to-hip ratio and overall shape, not muscle detail — practice finding the exact hip angle that makes your waist look smallest relative to your shoulders and hips.',
  ]},
  'Front Relaxed Pose': {icon:'quarterTurn', video:'figure front relaxed pose tutorial', points:[
    'Setup: stand facing forward, feet hip-width, weight evenly balanced, arms relaxed at your sides or lightly resting on your hips.',
    'Execution: engage a light, controlled full-body tone — noticeably more present than Bikini\'s fully relaxed look, but well short of a hard flex.',
    'Whole-body integration: keep shoulders open and down, ribcage lifted, and a slight, natural engagement through the abdomen and legs.',
    'Common mistake: either going too soft (looking under-conditioned) or too hard (flexing like a bodybuilding pose) — the sweet spot is controlled athletic tone.',
    'Judging insight: symmetry and proportion are being judged here as much as conditioning — a straight-on stance makes any left-right asymmetry obvious, so this is the pose to rehearse most in a mirror.',
  ]},
  'Rear Pose (Glute Emphasis)': {icon:'quarterTurn', video:'wellness division rear pose tutorial', points:[
    'Setup: turn your back to the judges, feet hip-width, weight balanced, one heel gently lifted for a subtle calf and glute line.',
    'Execution: engage your glutes and hamstrings with a light, controlled squeeze rather than a hard flex.',
    'Whole-body integration: keep your lower back neutral and shoulders relaxed but open — the whole point of this pose is to draw the eye to glute shape and leg lines, not the upper body.',
    'Common mistake: over-arching the lower back to try to push the glutes out further — this creates a strained look and can visually shorten the leg line instead of lengthening it.',
    'Judging insight: glute-hamstring tie-in (where the glute meets the top of the hamstring) is specifically what\'s being evaluated here — this responds directly to hip thrusts and Romanian deadlifts in training, more than squats alone.',
  ]},
  'Back Pose with Flex': {icon:'backDoubleBiceps', video:'wellness division back pose tutorial', points:[
    'Setup: back to the judges, feet shoulder-width, a light back-double-biceps-style arm position but without the muscular bodybuilding is intent.',
    'Execution: add a controlled, moderate flex through the back and glutes — noticeably more engaged than a fully relaxed quarter turn, but stopping well short of a maximal bodybuilding flex.',
    'Whole-body integration: keep the flex smooth and controlled rather than shaking or straining into it — composure is part of what\'s being judged.',
    'Common mistake: flexing as hard as a bodybuilding back pose. In this division that reads as overreaching for a look the class isn\'t judged on.',
    'Judging insight: this pose rewards visible shape and taper with a light contraction — think "back double biceps at 60% effort," not full intensity.',
  ]},
};

