const SWIM_STROKE_DETAILS = {
  'Breath Control & Rhythmic Bobbing': {icon:'bobbing', video:'swimming breath control bobbing drill for beginners', points:[
    'Setup: stand in chest-deep water, take a full breath, then submerge completely by bending the knees — this is the single most important skill in swimming, since almost every technique problem downstream traces back to a swimmer holding their breath and panicking.',
    'Execution: exhale slowly and fully through your nose and mouth while under water, then push off the bottom and inhale quickly through your mouth the instant your face clears the surface.',
    'Whole-body integration: keep the exhale continuous rather than holding air and blasting it out at the last second — a steady trickle of bubbles the entire time you\'re under is the actual skill, not the bob itself.',
    'Common mistake: holding the breath underwater and only exhaling right at the surface. This is the root cause of "breathless panic" in almost every anxious adult swimmer — fix this before touching any stroke technique.',
    'Coaching insight: repeat this 10-15 times per session until it\'s automatic and boring. Every stroke below assumes this rhythm already exists — trying to learn stroke timing while still holding your breath is trying to build on sand.',
  ]},
  'Front Float (Streamline Position)': {icon:'streamlineFront', video:'front float streamline position swimming tutorial', points:[
    'Setup: push off the wall or floor face-down with arms extended straight over head, one hand stacked on top of the other, biceps pressed against your ears.',
    'Execution: let your face drop into the water looking straight down (not forward), and let your hips and legs float up toward the surface — you\'re aiming to become as long, narrow, and flat as possible.',
    'Whole-body integration: this exact shape — the "streamline" — is the lowest-drag position the human body can hold in water, and it\'s the position you return to after every wall push-off, every stroke recovery, and every breath, for the rest of your swimming life.',
    'Common mistake: lifting the head to look forward. This single habit drops the hips and legs down into the water like an anchor, turning a nearly effortless float into a constant uphill fight — "swim downhill," as Total Immersion\'s Terry Laughlin put it, starts with where the head points.',
    'Coaching insight: practice holding this float for 5-10 seconds completely relaxed before adding any kick or pull — if you can\'t float here calmly, no amount of arm strength will fix the drag problem underneath it.',
  ]},
  'Back Float': {icon:'streamlineBack', video:'back float swimming tutorial beginners', points:[
    'Setup: lie back into the water with ears submerged, chin slightly tucked (not lifted), and arms resting at your sides or extended overhead.',
    'Execution: let your chest and hips rise while your lower legs stay relaxed and near the surface — pushing your belly button up toward the sky helps the hips float rather than sink.',
    'Whole-body integration: keep breathing normally and steadily; unlike the front float, your face is already clear of the water, so there\'s no breath-holding component here — this drill is purely about trusting buoyancy.',
    'Common mistake: lifting the head to check your position. Lifting the head drops the hips immediately, which is the exact opposite problem of front-float head position — in both floats, the head leads and the hips follow.',
    'Coaching insight: this is the foundation every backstroke swimmer returns to — if the back float feels unstable, backstroke will always feel like a fight against sinking hips rather than a smooth glide.',
  ]},
  'Flutter Kick on the Wall': {icon:'flutterKick', video:'flutter kick drill kickboard swimming tutorial', points:[
    'Setup: hold the wall or a kickboard with arms extended, body flat and horizontal, face in the water looking down.',
    'Execution: kick from the hip, not the knee — think of your legs as two long whips, with a small, quick flick at the ankle as the final snap, not a big bent-knee bicycle pedal.',
    'Whole-body integration: keep ankles loose and slightly pointed (plantarflexed); stiff, flexed ankles act like brakes and can cut kick propulsion by half even with identical leg effort.',
    'Common mistake: kicking from a bent knee like riding a bicycle. This is the most common flaw in beginners and wastes enormous energy for almost no forward propulsion — the power comes from the hip driving a mostly straight leg, with only a slight, natural knee bend.',
    'Coaching insight: a 6-beat kick (six kicks per full arm-stroke cycle) is the standard taught in competitive freestyle, but for a beginner just learning the motion, focus on ankle looseness and hip-driven rhythm long before worrying about counting beats.',
  ]},

  'Body Position & Streamline': {icon:'streamlineFront', video:'freestyle swimming body position technique', points:[
    'Setup: begin from the front float position — long, flat, face down, streamlined off the wall.',
    'Execution: keep your body as close to horizontal as possible with a slight natural downward slope from head to feet — a small amount of tilt is normal, but the goal is to minimize how much of your body is "plowing" through the water.',
    'Whole-body integration: the head is the rudder for the whole body — looking down (not forward) keeps the hips and legs riding high, which is the entire basis of Total Immersion\'s "swim downhill" cue for reducing drag.',
    'Common mistake: swimming with the head up to see forward. This is by far the most common cause of "sinking legs" in adult-learned freestyle — the fix is almost never a stronger kick, it\'s dropping the head.',
    'Coaching insight: before adding a single arm stroke, drill pure kicking in this position with a snorkel or by turning to breathe only occasionally — most technique flaws in freestyle are actually body-position flaws in disguise.',
  ]},
  'Flutter Kick': {icon:'flutterKick', video:'freestyle flutter kick technique tutorial', points:[
    'Setup: same hip-driven, straight-leg, loose-ankle kick as the fundamentals drill above, now performed continuously while swimming rather than holding a wall.',
    'Execution: keep the kick narrow — a kick that\'s too wide (more than roughly shoulder-width in amplitude) creates drag instead of propulsion, since it pushes water sideways instead of back.',
    'Whole-body integration: the kick\'s main job in freestyle is stabilizing body rotation and balance, not generating most of your speed — the majority of freestyle propulsion comes from the pull, so don\'t over-invest energy in a huge kick.',
    'Common mistake: kicking too hard and too early in a swim, burning out the legs in the first 50m and having nothing left for the pull. Reserve maximum kick effort for sprinting; distance swimming uses a light, efficient 2-beat or 6-beat kick.',
    'Coaching insight: a small, tight, fast kick from the hip beats a big, slow, bent-knee kick every time — this is exactly the same efficiency principle as a smaller, tighter boxing jab beating a big looping one.',
  ]},
  'Catch & Early Vertical Forearm': {icon:'armPullFreestyle', video:'freestyle high elbow catch early vertical forearm tutorial', points:[
    'Setup: as your hand enters the water in front of your shoulder (not crossing the midline), let it sink slightly before beginning any backward pull.',
    'Execution: bend at the elbow and rotate the forearm downward so it becomes vertical (pointing at the pool floor) while the elbow stays high — this "early vertical forearm" (EVF) lets your forearm and hand act as a paddle against still water instead of just pulling through water you\'ve already disturbed.',
    'Whole-body integration: this catch position — high elbow, vertical forearm, relaxed wrist — is the single technical element most responsible for the difference between an efficient swimmer and a splashy, tired one; it\'s the centerpiece of Sheila Taormina\'s technical coaching for age-group and masters swimmers in "Swim Speed Secrets".',
    'Common mistake: dropping the elbow so the whole arm sweeps straight back like an oar. This "dropped elbow" catch pulls mostly downward instead of backward, wasting the majority of the stroke\'s force on lifting the body instead of propelling it forward.',
    'Coaching insight: practice this slowly with a "catch-up drill" (one arm stays extended forward while the other completes a full stroke cycle) — slowing the stroke down is the only way to actually feel the difference between a high-elbow catch and a straight-arm pull.',
  ]},
  'Pull-Through & Finish (S-Pull)': {icon:'armPullFreestyle', video:'freestyle swimming s-pull finish technique tutorial', points:[
    'Setup: from the vertical-forearm catch, begin pulling your hand back along a gently curving path — in toward the body\'s midline, then back out and past the hip.',
    'Execution: accelerate the hand throughout the pull, finishing fastest right as it exits near the hip and thigh — a stroke that starts fast and fades is far less effective than one that builds speed to the finish.',
    'Whole-body integration: the S-shaped path (in, then back, then out) lets the hand and forearm stay pressed against "clean," undisturbed water for longer than a straight-line pull would, which is the actual mechanical reason it\'s faster despite covering a curved rather than direct path.',
    'Common mistake: pulling straight back in a rigid line and stopping the hand at the hip instead of finishing the push all the way through. A stroke that stops short leaves real propulsion on the table every single cycle.',
    'Coaching insight: think "push the pool behind you," not "pull yourself forward" — this subtle mental cue changes where swimmers apply force and consistently produces a stronger, more complete finish in coaching sessions.',
  ]},
  'High-Elbow Recovery': {icon:'armRecoveryHighElbow', video:'freestyle high elbow arm recovery technique', points:[
    'Setup: once your pull finishes near the hip, lift the elbow first, letting the forearm and hand hang relaxed and trail behind it as it swings forward over the water.',
    'Execution: keep the elbow noticeably higher than the hand throughout the recovery, with the fingertips grazing close to the water\'s surface rather than looping wide and high.',
    'Whole-body integration: a relaxed, high-elbow recovery lets the arm swing forward using momentum rather than muscular effort — this is where efficient swimmers actually "rest" mid-stroke, since the recovery arm should feel almost weightless.',
    'Common mistake: a straight, stiff-armed recovery that swings wide out to the side. Besides wasting energy, a wide, straight-arm recovery also throws off body rotation and balance, since the arm\'s weight is now off to the side instead of tracking straight over the shoulder.',
    'Coaching insight: a floppy, relaxed hand and wrist during recovery is a genuine skill, not laziness — tension anywhere in the recovery arm bleeds energy you\'ll want for the next catch and pull.',
  ]},
  'Bilateral (Rotary) Breathing': {icon:'breathingSide', video:'bilateral breathing freestyle swimming tutorial', points:[
    'Setup: rather than always breathing to the same side, rotate your whole body (not just your head) so your mouth clears the water in the trough created by that rotation.',
    'Execution: turn to breathe on a consistent rhythm — commonly every 3rd stroke, alternating sides — rather than every 2nd stroke to the same side every time.',
    'Whole-body integration: the breath should ride the natural body rotation already happening from the stroke, not require an independent head-lift — if you have to lift your head up and forward to breathe, the rotation underneath it isn\'t doing its job yet.',
    'Common mistake: lifting the head straight up to breathe instead of rotating to the side. A lifted head drops the hips and legs (the same "swim downhill" problem from body position) and breaks the stroke\'s rhythm every single breath.',
    'Coaching insight: bilateral breathing isn\'t just about symmetry — it forces even rotation to both sides, which is often what actually fixes a crooked, zig-zagging swim path more than any amount of "swim straight" cueing ever will.',
  ]},
  'Stroke Timing & Body Rotation': {icon:'rotationTiming', video:'freestyle body rotation timing swimming technique', points:[
    'Setup: as one arm catches and pulls, let your hips and shoulders rotate toward that side — freestyle is swum on your sides, alternating left and right, not flat on your stomach.',
    'Execution: rotation should originate from the core/hips, not just the shoulders twisting independently — the whole body rolls as a unit, roughly 30-45° to each side at a moderate pace.',
    'Whole-body integration: rotation does three jobs at once — it lets the recovery arm swing forward with less shoulder strain, it extends your reach on the catch for a longer, more powerful pull, and it\'s what makes bilateral breathing possible without lifting the head.',
    'Common mistake: swimming flat, with the shoulders and hips staying square to the bottom of the pool throughout. Flat freestyle looks like "windmilling" the arms and is both slower and far more shoulder-strain-prone than rotational freestyle.',
    'Coaching insight: this is genuinely the last piece to click for most self-taught swimmers — kick, catch, and recovery can all be individually correct and still look labored until rotation ties them together into one continuous, connected motion.',
  ]},

  'Back Float & Body Position': {icon:'streamlineBack', video:'backstroke body position technique tutorial', points:[
    'Setup: begin from the back float — ears submerged, hips up, chin slightly tucked rather than lifted.',
    'Execution: maintain a nearly flat, horizontal line from shoulders to ankles; a common reference cue is imagining a broomstick laid along your spine from head to tailbone.',
    'Whole-body integration: because your face is already out of the water, backstroke body position is purely about hip height — sinking hips is the single most common technical flaw and it\'s almost always caused by a lifted or overly tucked head or chin.',
    'Common mistake: lifting the head to see where you\'re going. This is even more costly in backstroke than freestyle, since it has no counterbalancing benefit (you can\'t see forward regardless) — it only drops the hips.',
    'Coaching insight: many swimmers benefit from picking a fixed point on the ceiling or gutter behind them and holding their gaze there — a still head is a stable head, and a stable head keeps the hips up.',
  ]},
  'Backstroke Flutter Kick': {icon:'flutterKick', video:'backstroke kick technique tutorial', points:[
    'Setup: same hip-driven, loose-ankle mechanics as freestyle flutter kick, now performed face-up with toes just barely breaking the surface on the upkick.',
    'Execution: the upkick (toes toward the sky) is the power phase in backstroke, the reverse emphasis from freestyle where the downkick generates more force — a common cue is "kick like you\'re squashing a bug on the surface with your big toe."',
    'Whole-body integration: a small, splashy fountain of white water just above the surface from the toes is actually a good sign in backstroke kick — it means the upkick is shallow and fast rather than deep and slow.',
    'Common mistake: a deep, exaggerated kick that pushes the knees above the surface. Knees breaking the surface create massive drag and usually means the kick is coming from the knee (a "bicycle kick") instead of the hip.',
    'Coaching insight: kickboard-on-the-chest backstroke kick drills, done regularly, build the ankle flexibility and hip-driven habit that most new backstrokers are missing entirely.',
  ]},
  'Catch & Pull': {icon:'backPull', video:'backstroke catch and pull technique tutorial', points:[
    'Setup: the recovering arm enters the water directly in line with the shoulder, pinky finger first, fingertips leading the entry with minimal splash.',
    'Execution: once submerged, bend the elbow to find a vertical-forearm catch (the same underlying principle as freestyle\'s EVF catch) and pull in a gently curving path down toward the hip, finishing with a small downward push of the palm at the very end.',
    'Whole-body integration: the pull\'s power comes from the shoulder and back rotating the body, not just the arm muscling water backward — this is why body rotation (below) and the pull are really one connected motion, not two separate skills.',
    'Common mistake: a straight-arm "windmill" pull with no elbow bend at all. Without the elbow bend and vertical forearm, the pulling surface is much smaller and the propulsion drops significantly, even though the motion looks similarly big and effortful.',
    'Coaching insight: because backstroke is the only competitive stroke swum without any visual reference to the catch, many swimmers develop a straight-arm pull simply because they can\'t see the mistake — drilling with a coach\'s feedback or underwater video is disproportionately valuable here.',
  ]},
  'Recovery Arm (Straight-Arm Exit & Entry)': {icon:'backRecovery', video:'backstroke arm recovery technique tutorial', points:[
    'Setup: as the pulling arm finishes near the hip, exit the water thumb-first with a fully straight arm.',
    'Execution: swing the straight arm in a continuous arc up and over to a position directly above the shoulder, then rotate the hand so it enters pinky-first, directly in line with that shoulder — not crossing over the head or drifting wide to the side.',
    'Whole-body integration: the straight-arm recovery (unlike freestyle\'s bent-elbow recovery) works because it\'s happening entirely above the water, where drag doesn\'t matter — trying to bend the elbow here just adds unnecessary muscular effort.',
    'Common mistake: entering the water directly overhead (in line with the ear) instead of the shoulder. An overhead entry twists the shoulder and torso awkwardly and usually throws off the whole-body rotation the stroke depends on.',
    'Coaching insight: picture a clock face lying flat with your head at 12 — the recovering arm should travel from roughly 6 (hip) to 12 (overhead) staying on that same vertical plane the entire way, never drifting toward 1 or 11.',
  ]},
  'Body Rotation & Timing': {icon:'rotationTiming', video:'backstroke body rotation timing technique', points:[
    'Setup: as one arm pulls and the other recovers, the whole body (hips and shoulders together) rotates toward the pulling-arm side, the same continuous-rotation principle as freestyle.',
    'Execution: backstroke uses a continuous alternating-arm timing — as one arm finishes its pull, the other should just be entering the water — with no pause or hitch between strokes.',
    'Whole-body integration: rotation in backstroke serves the same purposes as in freestyle — it lengthens the reach of the catching arm, helps clear the recovering arm from the water efficiently, and connects the kick, pull, and shoulders into one continuous system instead of separate parts.',
    'Common mistake: "flat" backstroke with no rotation, arms just windmilling over a rigid torso. This dramatically shortens stroke length and is a common habit among swimmers who learned backstroke last and never transferred the freestyle rotation feel over.',
    'Coaching insight: because there are always two arms in motion in backstroke (one pulling while the other recovers), timing errors are usually more obvious here than in freestyle — a coach watching from the side can immediately see if the entry and exit aren\'t synced.',
  ]},
  'Breathing Rhythm': {icon:'breathingSide', video:'backstroke breathing rhythm technique tutorial', points:[
    'Setup: since the face is already above water throughout backstroke, breathing isn\'t timed to surfacing the way it is in freestyle or breaststroke — it\'s purely about not accidentally holding your breath.',
    'Execution: establish a simple, fixed pattern — for example, inhale as one arm recovers, exhale as the other pulls — so breathing becomes automatic rather than an afterthought.',
    'Whole-body integration: a steady breathing rhythm keeps the whole stroke relaxed; holding the breath (a very common unconscious habit in backstroke specifically, since nothing forces an exhale) builds tension through the shoulders and neck that leaks into the pull and recovery.',
    'Common mistake: unconsciously holding the breath for several stroke cycles at a time, since there\'s no water forcing an exhale like in face-down strokes. This is uniquely a backstroke problem and worth deliberately checking for.',
    'Coaching insight: some coaches have swimmers count strokes out loud (quietly) during easy backstroke sets specifically to force a natural breathing pattern — an odd-sounding drill that reliably works.',
  ]},

  'Body Position & Streamline Glide': {icon:'streamlineFront', video:'breaststroke streamline glide technique tutorial', points:[
    'Setup: after each pull-kick cycle, breaststroke returns to a full front-float streamline — arms extended forward, legs together and straight, face down.',
    'Execution: hold this streamlined glide position longer than feels natural at first — breaststroke is unique among the four competitive strokes in having a genuine full-body pause built into its rhythm.',
    'Whole-body integration: this glide is where nearly all of breaststroke\'s efficiency comes from — a swimmer who rushes through the glide to start the next pull immediately is giving up the stroke\'s single biggest advantage over the other strokes.',
    'Common mistake: cutting the glide short out of impatience or anxiety. Beginners in particular tend to treat breaststroke like continuous paddling instead of a pull-breathe-kick-GLIDE cycle with a real pause at the end.',
    'Coaching insight: count "one-Mississippi" silently during the glide in early practice — most learners are shocked at how much longer the "correct" glide feels compared to what they were doing instinctively.',
  ]},
  'Breaststroke Pull (Out-Sweep, In-Sweep, Recovery)': {icon:'breastPull', video:'breaststroke pull technique tutorial', points:[
    'Setup: from the streamline, sweep both hands outward and slightly downward together in a shallow "Y" shape, palms angled to press against the water.',
    'Execution: once the hands reach roughly shoulder-width past the body, sweep them back inward and slightly down, tracing a heart-shaped or keyhole-shaped path that finishes with the hands coming together in front of the chest.',
    'Whole-body integration: this in-sweep is also what lifts the head and shoulders for the breath — the pull and the breath are one connected motion in breaststroke, not two separate actions happening to overlap.',
    'Common mistake: pulling too wide or too far back, past the shoulder line and toward the hips like a freestyle pull. A breaststroke pull that goes too far back kills the glide that should immediately follow it and adds drag instead of propulsion.',
    'Coaching insight: think "small and quick" for the pull, "long and slow" for the glide — this pull-glide contrast is the core rhythm every breaststroke swimmer needs to internalize, and most technical corrections in this stroke trace back to that ratio being off.',
  ]},
  'Whip Kick (Breaststroke Kick)': {icon:'whipKick', video:'breaststroke whip kick technique tutorial', points:[
    'Setup: from the streamlined glide, draw both heels up toward the glutes by bending the knees, keeping the knees roughly hip-width apart (narrower than they feel natural to beginners).',
    'Execution: rotate the feet outward (dorsiflex and turn out) at the ankles, then whip them out and around in a circular path before snapping them back together and straight, finishing in the streamline.',
    'Whole-body integration: the "whip" or "snap" at the very end — feet accelerating together and squeezing shut — is where nearly all the kick\'s propulsion actually comes from, similar to how a whip\'s cracking sound comes from the tip\'s final acceleration, not the handle\'s motion.',
    'Common mistake: the "bicycle kick" or "scissor kick," where the knees drop too wide and the legs kick in an up-and-down or scissoring motion instead of a circular whip. This is the single most common breaststroke error and is frequently disqualified in competitive swimming for exactly this reason.',
    'Coaching insight: practice this kick on your back initially so you can watch your own knees and feet — most swimmers are genuinely unaware their kick has drifted into a bicycle pattern until they can see it directly.',
  ]},
  'Timing: Pull-Breathe-Kick-Glide': {icon:'rotationTiming', video:'breaststroke timing pull breathe kick glide tutorial', points:[
    'Setup: the full breaststroke cycle follows a specific, non-overlapping sequence: pull, breathe, kick, then glide — often taught with the verbal cue "pull, breathe, kick, glide" said out loud in rhythm.',
    'Execution: the pull and breath happen together (as covered above), then as the arms recover forward into streamline, the kick fires — critically, the kick happens after the arms are already moving forward, not at the same time as the pull.',
    'Whole-body integration: this sequencing means at every point in the stroke, either the arms or the legs are providing propulsion, never neither and rarely both — a well-timed breaststroke has almost no "dead spots" despite having an obvious pause built into the glide.',
    'Common mistake: pulling and kicking at the same time (often from rushing or anxiety about the breath). This "flat" timing loses the sequential propulsion that makes breaststroke efficient and usually shows up as a choppy, tiring stroke.',
    'Coaching insight: slow-motion, exaggerated practice reps — counting each phase out loud — fixes timing errors faster than swimming at race pace ever will, since race pace hides timing flaws that show up clearly in slow motion.',
  ]},
  'Breathing & Head Position': {icon:'breastBreathing', video:'breaststroke breathing head position technique', points:[
    'Setup: the head lifts to breathe as a direct result of the in-sweep pull described above, not as an independent motion.',
    'Execution: lift only enough to clear the mouth and chin — the goal is the smallest possible head lift that still allows a breath, then the head drops back down as the arms extend forward into the glide.',
    'Whole-body integration: because the breath rides the pull\'s natural lift rather than requiring a separate motion, a well-timed breaststroke breath costs almost nothing in terms of extra drag or rhythm disruption.',
    'Common mistake: lifting the head early, before the pull\'s in-sweep has actually raised the shoulders and chest. An early head lift disconnects the breath from the stroke\'s natural rhythm and often drags the hips down as a result.',
    'Coaching insight: if you find yourself gasping or feeling rushed for air, the fix is almost always timing (breathing later, closer to when the pull naturally lifts you), not lifting the head higher or holding the breath longer.',
  ]},
  'Body Undulation (Modern Wave Breaststroke)': {icon:'undulationWave', video:'wave breaststroke undulation technique tutorial', points:[
    'Setup: once the fundamental pull-kick-glide timing above feels automatic, add a subtle forward-and-down press of the chest during the glide phase, the "wave" that defines modern elite breaststroke.',
    'Execution: as the arms extend forward into streamline, press the chest slightly downward and let the hips follow in a small, controlled wave, rather than staying perfectly flat and rigid.',
    'Whole-body integration: this undulation, refined by elite technicians in recent years, reduces the "stop-and-start" feel of classic flat breaststroke by keeping a small amount of continuous body motion even during the traditionally still glide phase.',
    'Common mistake: attempting this before the basic timing and kick are solid. Undulation added on top of a bicycle kick or mistimed pull just adds a new source of drag rather than the intended efficiency gain — this is genuinely an advanced refinement, not a beginner priority.',
    'Coaching insight: this is deliberately the last drill in the breaststroke progression here — a swimmer without the fundamentals in place should focus entirely on pull-breathe-kick-glide timing and a clean whip kick before ever adding a wave to the stroke.',
  ]},

  'Body Undulation (The Wave)': {icon:'undulationWave', video:'butterfly stroke body undulation wave technique', points:[
    'Setup: from a front streamline, initiate a wave-like motion starting at the chest — press the chest down and slightly forward, which sends a ripple of motion down through the hips and into the legs.',
    'Execution: the wave should travel through the body in sequence — chest, then hips, then legs — like a whip cracking, rather than the whole body moving as one rigid, stiff unit.',
    'Whole-body integration: this undulation is the actual engine of butterfly — nearly every other element (the kick, the pull, the breathing) times itself around this wave rather than the wave being an add-on to an otherwise normal stroke.',
    'Common mistake: trying to "muscle" butterfly using only the arms, with a stiff, flat torso. This is exhausting and looks and feels nothing like real butterfly — the stroke has a reputation for being brutally hard largely because it\'s so often taught (or self-taught) without this wave foundation.',
    'Coaching insight: practice this undulation with a kickboard held at arm\'s length, or even just floating and "worming" through the water with no kick or pull at all — feeling the wave in isolation is the fastest path to a butterfly that doesn\'t feel like fighting the water.',
  ]},
  'Two-Beat Dolphin Kick': {icon:'dolphinKick', video:'butterfly dolphin kick technique tutorial', points:[
    'Setup: legs together, moving as a single unit (not alternating like flutter kick) — think of a mermaid\'s tail or a dolphin\'s fluke, not two independent legs.',
    'Execution: two kicks occur per arm-stroke cycle — one small kick as the hands enter the water and press down at the start of the pull, and one larger kick as the hands finish the pull and push down at the hips.',
    'Whole-body integration: the kick isn\'t a separate action layered on top of the undulation — it\'s the same wave motion continuing down through the legs, timed specifically to reinforce the two key power moments of the arm stroke.',
    'Common mistake: kicking from the knees only, in a stiff, choppy motion disconnected from the body wave. A dolphin kick powered purely by the knees looks and feels completely different from one that starts as a wave through the torso and simply continues into the legs.',
    'Coaching insight: many coaches teach dolphin kick on the swimmer\'s back first (face-up, watching your own legs) before combining it with the pull, exactly the same logic as the on-the-back breaststroke kick drill — seeing the motion accelerates learning it.',
  ]},
  'Butterfly Pull (Keyhole Pattern)': {icon:'flyPull', video:'butterfly stroke keyhole pull technique tutorial', points:[
    'Setup: both hands enter the water together in front of the shoulders (not crossing past the body\'s midline), then press outward and down slightly, similar in principle to breaststroke\'s early out-sweep.',
    'Execution: from that outward press, both hands sweep back inward toward the body\'s centerline, then curve back outward again toward the hips — tracing a shape that looks like a keyhole or hourglass viewed from below.',
    'Whole-body integration: this is fundamentally the same high-elbow, vertical-forearm catch principle used in freestyle, just performed symmetrically with both arms at once instead of alternating.',
    'Common mistake: pulling in a straight line front-to-back instead of the curving keyhole path. A straight pull loses the mechanical advantage of pressing against undisturbed water at each phase, the same underlying issue as a dropped-elbow freestyle catch.',
    'Coaching insight: because both arms move symmetrically and simultaneously, any pull-timing error in butterfly is immediately doubled compared to an alternating stroke — small technical flaws here are unusually costly to overall efficiency.',
  ]},
  'Butterfly Recovery': {icon:'flyRecovery', video:'butterfly stroke arm recovery technique tutorial', points:[
    'Setup: as the pull finishes near the hips, both arms sweep out and around low over the water\'s surface simultaneously, elbows only slightly bent.',
    'Execution: keep the arms relaxed and let momentum, not muscular effort, carry them forward in a wide arc back to the entry point in front of the shoulders.',
    'Whole-body integration: a relaxed recovery is where efficient butterfly swimmers actually recover energy mid-stroke, exactly as in freestyle — tension in the recovery arms is one of the fastest ways to turn butterfly into the exhausting stroke of its reputation.',
    'Common mistake: muscling the arms high and stiff over the water, or rushing the recovery to "catch up" to the kick timing. Both waste energy that should be saved for the pull and kick, which are what actually move the swimmer forward.',
    'Coaching insight: a common cue is "throw your arms, don\'t lift them" — the image of tossing both arms forward rather than hoisting them tends to produce the relaxed, momentum-driven recovery that\'s actually correct.',
  ]},
  'Breathing (Chin-Skim Technique)': {icon:'flyBreathing', video:'butterfly stroke breathing technique tutorial', points:[
    'Setup: breathing is timed to the second, larger dolphin kick and the finish of the pull, when the body\'s natural wave already lifts the chest and head slightly.',
    'Execution: lift only enough for the chin to skim the water\'s surface — an image many coaches use directly — rather than lifting the whole head and shoulders high out of the water.',
    'Whole-body integration: because the breath rides the same wave that\'s already lifting the body at that point in the cycle, a well-timed breath adds very little extra drag or effort, similar in principle to breaststroke\'s pull-timed breath.',
    'Common mistake: lifting the head too high, too early, or on every single stroke cycle. Over-lifting stalls the forward wave motion and is a major reason butterfly feels unsustainable for swimmers who haven\'t yet timed the breath to the stroke\'s natural rhythm.',
    'Coaching insight: many competitive swimmers breathe only every second stroke cycle rather than every single one, specifically to preserve the wave\'s momentum — breathing less often, at the right moment, often feels easier than breathing every stroke at the wrong moment.',
  ]},
  'Timing & Rhythm': {icon:'rotationTiming', video:'butterfly stroke timing rhythm technique tutorial', points:[
    'Setup: butterfly\'s full cycle is: kick 1 as hands enter and press down, pull through the keyhole pattern, kick 2 as the pull finishes near the hips, then the relaxed recovery back to entry.',
    'Execution: the entire stroke should feel like one continuous wave passing through the body from head to toe, not four separate, disconnected actions performed back-to-back.',
    'Whole-body integration: this is the stroke where "less is more" is most true of all four strokes — swimmers who try to power through butterfly with raw strength alone almost always tire within a lap or two, while swimmers who find the timing can sustain it far longer with noticeably less effort.',
    'Common mistake: over-kicking, over-pulling, or rushing the recovery in an attempt to "muscle" the stroke, especially under fatigue. This is almost always a timing problem being misdiagnosed as a strength problem.',
    'Coaching insight: if butterfly consistently feels exhausting after only a few strokes, the fix is virtually never "get stronger" — it\'s almost always "slow down and find the wave," since a poorly timed stroke wastes far more energy fighting itself than a well-timed one spends moving forward.',
  ]},
};

