const CLIMB_TECHNIQUE_DETAILS = {
  'Three Points of Contact': {icon:'threePoints', video:'climbing three points of contact technique beginners', points:[
    'Setup: before moving any single hand or foot, check that the other three limbs are already solidly placed — this is the default state you return to between every individual move.',
    'Execution: move only one limb at a time, keeping the remaining three in stable contact with the wall while that limb repositions to its next hold.',
    'Whole-body integration: this isn\'t a rigid rule for absolute beginners only — even elite climbers are constantly cycling through three-point stability between dynamic moves; the difference is speed and confidence, not skipping the principle.',
    'Common mistake: reaching for the next hold while already off-balance on only one or two points of contact, then grabbing and pulling to "catch" the balance rather than moving from a stable base.',
    'Coaching insight: new climbers often think climbing is about arm strength because they\'re gripping too hard while off-balance — a huge amount of perceived "grip fatigue" in beginners is actually a balance and stability problem being compensated for with the hands.',
  ]},
  'Straight-Arm Resting (Skeletal Hang)': {icon:'straightArmHang', video:'climbing straight arm technique resting skeleton', points:[
    'Setup: whenever holds allow it, hang from a straight (fully extended) arm rather than a bent one, letting your skeleton — not your bicep and forearm muscles — bear the load.',
    'Execution: actively relax the bent-arm muscles once the arm is extended; a straight elbow transfers tension into the bone and connective tissue, which doesn\'t fatigue the way muscle does.',
    'Whole-body integration: this is the single biggest lever new climbers have for endurance — climbers who keep both arms bent throughout a route pump out dramatically faster than those who straighten and shake out whenever possible.',
    'Common mistake: keeping the arms chronically bent "just in case," out of a fear of falling. This is almost always a confidence problem rather than a strength problem, and it\'s the most common reason new climbers gas out on routes well within their physical capability.',
    'Coaching insight: consciously look for a bent elbow anywhere it isn\'t needed and straighten it — this single habit, repeated on every climb, does more for endurance than months of extra pull-up training.',
  ]},
  'Hip Positioning & Wall Awareness': {icon:'hipPosition', video:'climbing hip positioning technique wall awareness', points:[
    'Setup: rather than facing the wall square-on, rotate your hip toward the wall on the same side as the hand or foot you\'re reaching with.',
    'Execution: turning the hip in brings your center of gravity closer to the wall and extends your reach significantly further than a flat, square-on position ever could.',
    'Whole-body integration: this hip rotation is the foundation underneath several named techniques below (flagging, backstepping, drop-knees) — they\'re really all variations on the same core idea of turning the hips to change your reach and balance.',
    'Common mistake: staying square to the wall on steep or technical terrain, which shortens your effective reach and forces the arms to compensate for what should be a hip and footwork problem.',
    'Coaching insight: Dave MacLeod\'s widely read coaching book "9 Out of 10 Climbers Make the Same Mistakes" makes exactly this point — most plateaued climbers have a technique and body-positioning gap, not a strength gap, and hip awareness is usually the first thing to fix.',
  ]},
  'Quiet Feet & Precision Footwork': {icon:'quietFeet', video:'climbing quiet feet precision footwork drill', points:[
    'Setup: look at the foothold before moving your foot there, and place your foot exactly where you looked — no searching or scraping around once it\'s near the hold.',
    'Execution: place the foot down with control and stop it there; a foot that lands and immediately settles without shifting or re-adjusting is the actual goal, not just "getting it on the hold eventually."',
    'Whole-body integration: precise footwork directly reduces grip fatigue, since sloppy feet force the arms to compensate for lost balance — this is one of the clearest examples of how climbing technique and endurance are the same skill viewed from different angles.',
    'Common mistake: "feeling around" for a foothold with the toe after placing it, or stepping loudly and imprecisely. A loud, scrabbling foot placement is a reliable sign the eyes didn\'t do the work before the foot moved.',
    'Coaching insight: a classic coaching drill is climbing an easy, familiar route while trying to place each foot completely silently — it\'s a deceptively hard drill that exposes footwork sloppiness fast, even for climbers who feel technically solid.',
  ]},

  'Crimp Grip (Full & Half Crimp)': {icon:'crimpGrip', video:'climbing crimp grip technique full half crimp', points:[
    'Setup: fingers bent sharply at the middle knuckle, gripping a small edge with the fingertips and first pad — in a "full crimp," the thumb locks down over the top of the index finger for extra support; in a "half crimp," the thumb stays off to the side.',
    'Execution: keep the wrist relatively straight and let the fingers, not the wrist, do the gripping work; full crimp adds real strength on very small edges but concentrates far more force through the finger joints and the A2 pulley tendon specifically.',
    'Whole-body integration: crimping is a finger-and-forearm-intensive grip, so pair it with good body positioning (hips in, straight arms elsewhere) so the fingers aren\'t also compensating for poor balance on top of an already demanding grip.',
    'Common mistake: defaulting to a full crimp on every small edge out of habit, even when an open-hand grip (below) would work almost as well with far less joint stress. Climbing medicine specialists, including sports physicians like Dr. Volker Schöffl who has published extensively on climbing finger injuries, specifically flag full-crimp loading — especially under dynamic or high-load movement — as a major risk factor for A2 pulley strains and tears.',
    'Coaching insight: build half-crimp and open-hand strength as your default, and treat full crimp as a specific tool for genuinely tiny edges rather than an automatic habit — this single adjustment is one of the most effective ways climbers reduce finger injury risk over a long career.',
  ]},
  'Open Hand (Drag) Grip': {icon:'openHandGrip', video:'climbing open hand drag grip technique', points:[
    'Setup: fingers curve over the hold in a shallow, relaxed arc without sharply bending at the middle knuckle — the hand looks almost like it\'s gently dragging across the hold rather than clawing it.',
    'Execution: let the hold\'s shape and friction do more of the work than the fingers\' flexor tension; this grip relies more on the skin and larger finger pads than on maximal joint torque.',
    'Whole-body integration: open hand is generally considered the safer default grip for the finger joints and pulley tendons, since it distributes load more evenly and avoids concentrating force at the sharply bent middle joint the way a full crimp does.',
    'Common mistake: assuming open hand is simply "weaker" and avoiding it in favor of crimping everything. On many hold shapes, especially rounded or incut edges, a well-trained open-hand grip is nearly as strong as a crimp while carrying meaningfully less injury risk.',
    'Coaching insight: many structured climbing training plans, including Eric Hörst\'s widely used "Training for Climbing" progressions, deliberately build open-hand strength early and specifically, precisely because it\'s the grip most climbers under-train relative to how often it\'s actually useful on real routes.',
  ]},
  'Pinch Grip': {icon:'pinchGrip', video:'climbing pinch grip technique tutorial', points:[
    'Setup: thumb pressing directly against the fingers on the opposite side of a hold, squeezing the hold between them like gripping a brick or a block.',
    'Execution: generate force by actively squeezing the thumb and fingers toward each other, rather than just hanging passively on the hold the way you might on a crimp or jug.',
    'Whole-body integration: pinch strength comes disproportionately from the thumb and the muscles at the base of it, which most other grips barely use — this is why pinch-specific training (block pinches, pinch blocks on a hangboard) is usually a separate line item in structured climbing training.',
    'Common mistake: relying purely on finger strength and forgetting to actively engage the thumb, which turns a pinch into a much weaker, harder-to-control version of an open-hand grip.',
    'Coaching insight: pinch holds often show up on volumes and comp-style features as much as on outdoor rock, and are a common weak point for climbers who\'ve trained mostly on crimpy, edge-heavy terrain — worth deliberately seeking out if your local gym has pinch-heavy problems.',
  ]},
  'Sloper': {icon:'sloperGrip', video:'climbing sloper technique friction grip', points:[
    'Setup: place as much of the palm and fingers in full, flat contact with the rounded hold as possible — there\'s no edge to hook onto, so surface area and friction are the entire game.',
    'Execution: keep the wrist low and the elbow slightly bent to maximize downward pressure into the hold, and keep body weight low and centered directly under the hold rather than reaching up and out from it.',
    'Whole-body integration: slopers reward good body positioning more than almost any other hold type — a climber with poor hip and weight positioning will slide right off a sloper that a well-positioned climber holds easily with the same forearm strength.',
    'Common mistake: gripping too hard with the fingers, which actually reduces palm contact and friction. Slopers respond better to a relaxed, broad palm press than to a tense, clawing grip.',
    'Coaching insight: chalk, skin temperature, and rock/hold temperature all matter disproportionately on slopers compared to edges or crimps — this is a grip type where external conditions can change difficulty almost as much as technique does.',
  ]},
  'Pocket Grip': {icon:'pocketGrip', video:'climbing pocket grip technique two finger mono', points:[
    'Setup: insert one, two, or three fingers into a hole or shallow pocket in the rock or wall, choosing the finger combination the pocket\'s shape and spacing actually supports.',
    'Execution: keep the inserted fingers in a relatively natural, only slightly curled position rather than jamming them in and cranking at a sharp angle, which concentrates enormous stress on individual finger joints.',
    'Whole-body integration: pockets — especially two-finger and mono (one-finger) pockets — carry meaningfully higher injury risk per unit of force than open-hand or even crimp grips, because the load concentrates on so few tendons and joints at once.',
    'Common mistake: cranking hard on a mono or two-finger pocket cold, without a proper warm-up, or using pockets as a primary strength-training tool too early in a climbing career. This is one of the more common ways climbers develop serious finger tendon injuries.',
    'Coaching insight: build general finger strength on jugs, edges, and open-hand grips first, and treat pocket-specific training as an advanced addition once a solid strength base and a few years of healthy tendon loading are already in place — this is standard, widely repeated advice across structured climbing training programs precisely because pocket injuries are so common in climbers who skip that progression.',
  ]},
  'Gaston': {icon:'gastonGrip', video:'climbing gaston technique tutorial', points:[
    'Setup: grip a vertical-ish edge or feature with the thumb pointing down and the elbow out to the side, then push (rather than pull) outward and away from your body.',
    'Execution: think of the motion as prying a door open from the inside — the shoulder and upper back drive the outward press, with the arm mostly transmitting force rather than generating it independently.',
    'Whole-body integration: a gaston is fundamentally a shoulder-and-back move, not a hand-strength move; poor shoulder positioning or a collapsed chest makes a gaston feel far harder than the hold itself would otherwise justify.',
    'Common mistake: trying to muscle a gaston with the arm and hand alone, elbow tucked in close to the body. Tucking the elbow removes the mechanical leverage that makes a gaston work in the first place.',
    'Coaching insight: gastons are one of the more shoulder-intensive techniques in climbing — the antagonist and shoulder-health work already built into the Rock Climbing program\'s dryland training (band external rotation, scapular retraction, face pulls) directly supports safer, stronger gaston technique on the wall.',
  ]},
  'Undercling': {icon:'underclingGrip', video:'climbing undercling technique tutorial', points:[
    'Setup: grip a hold from underneath, palm facing up, fingers curling over the underside of the feature rather than over the top of it.',
    'Execution: keep your body weight low and your feet positioned relatively high and close to your hands, since underclings work by pulling up and in against the hold rather than down on top of it.',
    'Whole-body integration: underclings depend heavily on foot position — if the feet are too low or too far from the wall, the body\'s center of gravity ends up in the wrong place relative to the pull direction, and the hold feels far worse than its actual quality.',
    'Common mistake: keeping the feet low and reaching upward from an extended, straight-leg position. This pulls the hips away from the wall exactly when they need to stay close, and turns a manageable undercling into a strength battle.',
    'Coaching insight: a common cue is "high feet, low hips" for underclings — get the feet up first, then commit to the pull, rather than committing to the pull and hoping the feet catch up.',
  ]},
  'Side Pull': {icon:'sidepullGrip', video:'climbing side pull technique tutorial', points:[
    'Setup: grip a hold oriented vertically (or near-vertically), with the pulling force directed sideways, back toward your own body, rather than straight down.',
    'Execution: this grip almost always needs to be paired with opposing footwork — flagging a leg out to the opposite side, or pressing a foot out wide — to create the counter-tension that keeps the body from simply swinging off the wall.',
    'Whole-body integration: a side pull in isolation, without the right foot counter-pressure, tends to spin the climber\'s body away from the wall — the hold and the footwork are really one combined technique, not two separate decisions.',
    'Common mistake: grabbing a side pull and pulling straight in toward the body without setting up the opposing foot or flag first, which usually results in a barn-door swing off the wall.',
    'Coaching insight: side pulls are a great example of why "the fix is often in the feet, not the hands" — if a side pull feels impossible to hold, look at what the feet and hips are doing before assuming the grip itself is the problem.',
  ]},

  'Edging': {icon:'edgingFoot', video:'climbing edging footwork technique tutorial', points:[
    'Setup: place the inside or outside edge of the climbing shoe precisely on a small foothold, using the stiffer, more precise edge of the shoe rather than the flatter sole.',
    'Execution: apply weight straight down through that precise contact point and trust it fully once placed, rather than shifting or testing the foot repeatedly after it\'s down.',
    'Whole-body integration: edging rewards the same precision and confidence covered in "Quiet Feet" above — a foot that\'s placed with real commitment holds far better than the same foot placed tentatively on the exact same hold.',
    'Common mistake: placing the foot too low on the shoe (near the arch) instead of on the precise point of the big toe or the shoe\'s edge, which reduces both precision and the leverage available to push off the hold.',
    'Coaching insight: shoe choice matters here more than almost any other technique — a stiffer-soled shoe makes edging noticeably easier for less-experienced climbers, while very soft, aggressive shoes demand more foot strength and precision to edge well.',
  ]},
  'Smearing': {icon:'smearingFoot', video:'climbing smearing footwork technique tutorial', points:[
    'Setup: press as much of the shoe\'s rubber sole flat against a feature-less or low-angle section of rock or wall as possible, rather than looking for a distinct edge that isn\'t there.',
    'Execution: keep the heel low (often below the level of the toes) to maximize the amount of rubber in contact with the surface, and trust friction rather than a mechanical edge to hold the foot in place.',
    'Whole-body integration: smearing depends heavily on keeping body weight directly over the foot — leaning away from a smear, the same mistake that ruins slopers, breaks the friction contact and the foot slides immediately.',
    'Common mistake: raising the heel and trying to stand on the toe like an edge, when the surface genuinely has no edge to use. This reduces the contact patch exactly when maximum surface area is what\'s needed.',
    'Coaching insight: smearing is often the technique climbers trust least and therefore practice least, since it feels precarious compared to a solid edge — deliberately seeking out low-angle, featureless terrain to practice on is the fastest way to build genuine trust in the technique.',
  ]},
  'Backstep & Drop Knee': {icon:'dropKnee', video:'climbing backstep drop knee technique tutorial', points:[
    'Setup: turn one hip into the wall and place the outside edge of that same-side foot on a hold, rather than the usual inside edge — this is the "backstep."',
    'Execution: from a backstep, dropping that same knee downward and in toward the wall (the "drop knee") rotates the hip even further into the wall, extending the opposite-side reach significantly.',
    'Whole-body integration: this is one of the clearest applications of the hip-positioning principle covered above — the backstep and drop-knee exist specifically to change hip orientation and extend reach on steep or technical terrain.',
    'Common mistake: attempting a drop-knee with the standing leg\'s hip still square to the wall, which limits the knee\'s rotation and often creates uncomfortable, ineffective torque at the hip joint instead of the intended reach extension.',
    'Coaching insight: this technique is genuinely more about hip mobility and rotation than raw leg strength — climbers who struggle with drop-knees often benefit as much from hip mobility work as from any climbing-specific drilling.',
  ]},
  'Flagging': {icon:'flagging', video:'climbing flagging technique tutorial', points:[
    'Setup: extend one leg out to the side (or occasionally behind the other leg) without placing it on any hold at all, using it purely as a counterbalance.',
    'Execution: the flagged leg\'s job is to shift your center of gravity back over your base of support, specifically to prevent the "barn door" swing that happens when reaching to one side with only one foot on the wall.',
    'Whole-body integration: flagging is often the missing piece that makes an otherwise-impossible-feeling reach suddenly manageable — it\'s frequently paired with side pulls and gastons for exactly this counterbalancing reason.',
    'Common mistake: not flagging at all when reaching across the body to one side, and instead trying to muscle through the resulting instability with a harder grip. A well-timed flag often does more for a hard reach than any amount of extra hand strength.',
    'Coaching insight: try the same reach with and without a flag on an easy route — the difference is usually dramatic and immediate, which is why flagging is one of the first "aha" moments in most climbers\' technical development.',
  ]},

  'Heel Hook': {icon:'heelHook', video:'climbing heel hook technique tutorial', points:[
    'Setup: hook the heel of the shoe over a hold or feature — commonly above waist height, on steep terrain, or on features like arêtes and roofs — rather than standing on it with the toe.',
    'Execution: actively pull with the hooked heel by contracting the hamstring, as if trying to drag the heel back toward your glutes; a passive heel hook that isn\'t actively engaged provides far less stability than one being actively pulled.',
    'Whole-body integration: a well-executed heel hook effectively adds a "third arm" to the body\'s points of contact, taking significant load off the hands on steep terrain — this is why heel hooking is disproportionately important on overhanging routes.',
    'Common mistake: placing the heel passively without engaging the hamstring to actively pull, which leaves the foot vulnerable to popping off the hold under load exactly when it\'s needed most.',
    'Coaching insight: heel hooking uses the hamstring and hip in a way most other lower-body training doesn\'t address — climbers who heel hook frequently often benefit from specific hamstring and hip-flexor conditioning alongside their regular pulling and finger training.',
  ]},
  'Toe Hook': {icon:'toeHook', video:'climbing toe hook technique tutorial', points:[
    'Setup: hook the top of the foot/toe (rather than the sole) underneath a hold, feature, or roof lip, typically to prevent the body from swinging outward away from the wall.',
    'Execution: actively pull the hooked toe upward and back toward the shin, engaging the muscles on the top of the foot and shin rather than leaving the foot passively caught under the feature.',
    'Whole-body integration: toe hooks are most common on roofs and steep, technical terrain where they work as a counter-tension anchor against a reaching hand move, similar in principle to how a heel hook opposes a pulling hand.',
    'Common mistake: relying on the toe hook passively "catching" the body rather than actively engaging it, which tends to fail exactly at the moment of a dynamic reach when the tension is highest.',
    'Coaching insight: toe hooks are less commonly drilled deliberately than heel hooks, simply because they come up less often — but on steep, roof-heavy terrain specifically, they\'re frequently the difference between a move being possible or not, so it\'s worth seeking out roof problems to build comfort with them.',
  ]},
  'Mantling': {icon:'mantling', video:'climbing mantle technique tutorial', points:[
    'Setup: from a hold or ledge at roughly chest-to-shoulder height, press down on it with one or both hands, similar to the motion of pushing yourself up and out of a swimming pool.',
    'Execution: as the arms straighten and push the body upward, get a foot up onto the same ledge or a nearby foothold as early as possible, transferring weight from the arms to the leg as soon as it\'s viable.',
    'Whole-body integration: mantling briefly resembles a pressing movement (like a dip or push-up) more than the pulling motion most of climbing relies on — it\'s a genuinely different muscular pattern and often catches climbers with strong pulling strength but underdeveloped pressing strength off guard.',
    'Common mistake: trying to press all the way up on the arms alone without getting a foot up early, which demands far more shoulder and triceps strength than necessary and often stalls out at the hardest point of the press.',
    'Coaching insight: because mantling is a pressing pattern, general upper-body pressing work (push-ups, dips, shoulder press) — even though it isn\'t part of the typical pulling-dominant climbing training split — pays real dividends specifically for mantle-heavy routes and boulder problems.',
  ]},
  'Stemming': {icon:'stemming', video:'climbing stemming technique tutorial', points:[
    'Setup: push the hands and/or feet outward against two opposing surfaces at the same time — most commonly the two walls of a corner (dihedral) or a wide chimney.',
    'Execution: generate stability by actively pushing the limbs apart and outward into both surfaces simultaneously, rather than relying on any single hold — the tension between the two opposing pushes is what creates the stability.',
    'Whole-body integration: stemming can be remarkably rest-friendly once set up correctly, since the legs (not the arms) do most of the load-bearing work — many climbers find genuine, low-effort rests in a stem position mid-route.',
    'Common mistake: standing too upright and square in a stem, rather than actively pushing the feet outward and apart. A passive, narrow stance in a corner provides far less stability than a wide, actively-pushed one.',
    'Coaching insight: because stemming relies so heavily on hip mobility and the ability to spread the legs wide while maintaining control, general hip and adductor mobility work translates directly and noticeably into more comfortable, secure stemming on the wall.',
  ]},
  'Dynamic Movement (Dyno)': {icon:'dyno', video:'climbing dyno technique tutorial', points:[
    'Setup: identify a target hold that\'s out of static reach even with good hip positioning and flagging, confirming a dynamic (jumping) move is genuinely necessary rather than a technique gap.',
    'Execution: generate momentum from a coordinated push through the legs and a pull through the arms simultaneously, timing the release and catch of the target hold at the peak of that generated momentum.',
    'Whole-body integration: a controlled dyno is a full-body power movement, much closer in character to a vertical jump or an Olympic lift than to controlled climbing — the legs, not the arms, generate the majority of the upward force even though the hands do the final catching.',
    'Common mistake: trying to muscle a "dyno" purely with the arms while barely engaging the legs, or attempting dynamic moves before static, controlled movement and body positioning are actually solid — dynamic technique amplifies whatever positioning is already present, good or bad.',
    'Coaching insight: because a dyno demands snappy hip and leg drive, it\'s a technique that benefits directly from general explosive-power training — this is part of why structured climbing programs (including the Rock Climbing program\'s own pulling-strength and core-tension days) build a general strength base before dynamic-movement-specific practice.',
  ]},
  'Rock-over': {icon:'rockover', video:'climbing rockover technique tutorial', points:[
    'Setup: with one foot placed relatively high on a foothold, shift your body weight directly over that foot rather than pulling upward primarily with the arms.',
    'Execution: let the hips lead the movement, rotating and rolling over the high foot until your center of gravity is stacked above it, then stand up through that leg to gain height.',
    'Whole-body integration: a clean rock-over is fundamentally a hip-driven, leg-powered movement — climbers who instead try to pull themselves up and over with the arms are doing what should be a leg-strength move as an arm-strength move, which is both less efficient and more fatiguing.',
    'Common mistake: leaning away from the high foot instead of committing weight directly over it, which keeps the center of gravity in the wrong place and makes the move feel far more strenuous than it needs to.',
    'Coaching insight: rock-overs are one of the clearest illustrations of climbing\'s core efficiency principle — using big, strong muscle groups (the legs) to do work that tired, smaller muscles (the fingers and forearms) would otherwise be asked to do instead.',
  ]},
};

