const GRAPPLE_TECHNIQUE_DETAILS = {
  'Hip Escape (Shrimping)': {icon:'hipEscape', video:'bjj hip escape shrimping technique tutorial', points:[
    'Setup: lying on your back, post on one hand and the opposite foot, with the other leg free to move.',
    'Execution: push through the post to shoot your hips backward and away, creating space on that side, then immediately recover the near knee back in toward your frame before repeating on the other side.',
    'Whole-body integration: this single movement — creating space with the hips, then recovering — underlies almost every guard retention and escape in the sport; it\'s drilled in isolation for exactly that reason.',
    'Common mistake: shrimping with the arms instead of the legs and hips, or forgetting to recover the knee, which leaves the space you just created open for the top person to fill right back in.',
    'Coaching insight: most white belts over-rely on upper-body strength to create space; hip escape is usually the first drill that teaches the hips, not the arms, are the actual engine of bottom-position movement in BJJ.',
  ]},
  'Bridge & Roll (Upa Escape)': {icon:'bridgeRoll', video:'bjj upa bridge roll mount escape tutorial', points:[
    'Setup: from bottom mount, trap one of the top person\'s arms and the same-side leg, then plant the same-side foot close to your hip.',
    'Execution: bridge explosively up and over that trapped shoulder, driving the bridge with the hips and trapped-side leg together, timing the roll to the very top of the bridge rather than muscling it from flat on the back.',
    'Whole-body integration: this is the fundamental mount escape taught in essentially every BJJ curriculum, precisely because bottom mount is one of the most disadvantageous positions in the sport and needs a reliable answer.',
    'Common mistake: bridging straight up without first trapping the arm and leg, which just lets the top person post out and ride the bridge instead of being rolled by it.',
    'Coaching insight: the bridge itself is a leg-and-hip-driven movement, not an ab crunch — a bridge powered mostly through the torso rarely generates enough force to actually roll a resisting opponent.',
  ]},
  'Technical Stand-up': {icon:'technicalStandup', video:'bjj technical stand up tutorial', points:[
    'Setup: from the ground, post one hand behind you and bring the opposite foot underneath your hips, keeping the other leg extended out in front for base.',
    'Execution: stand up through the posted hand and planted foot without ever fully turning your back to whoever is in front of you.',
    'Whole-body integration: this is the standard safe way to return to standing from the ground while still facing — and defending against — an opponent, rather than turning away blind.',
    'Common mistake: standing up by turning and pushing off the knees first, which briefly takes the eyes and hands off the opponent at exactly the moment they\'re most able to attack.',
    'Coaching insight: this stand-up is drilled so often early on because it\'s the connective tissue between ground grappling and getting back to a neutral, safe position — it shows up constantly outside of pure BJJ too, including self-defense contexts.',
  ]},
  'Frames & Guard Retention': {icon:'framesRetention', video:'bjj frames guard retention drill', points:[
    'Setup: on your back with a partner working to pass, use your arms as frames — braced against their hips, biceps, or legs — rather than gripping cloth or limbs directly.',
    'Execution: as they move to pass, use hip escapes and the frames together to constantly re-center your hips between you and them, prioritizing re-establishing distance the instant guard is challenged.',
    'Whole-body integration: guard retention is trained here as its own standalone skill, separate from any specific guard style, since the underlying hip movement and framing are shared across nearly all of them.',
    'Common mistake: waiting until the guard is already fully passed to react, instead of treating the very first inch of pressure as the cue to frame and shrimp.',
    'Coaching insight: strong frames rely on bone structure and posture, not raw arm strength — a smaller grappler with good frames can stall a much stronger passer, which is why this is taught before any specific guard.',
  ]},
  'Knee Slice Pass': {icon:'kneeSlicePass', video:'bjj knee slice pass technique tutorial', points:[
    'Setup: control the opponent\'s far-side hip or leg with your hands to prevent them re-guarding, then align to drive one knee toward their center line.',
    'Execution: slide that knee through past their hip and thigh while keeping your posture heavy and your weight pressing down through the sliding knee, rather than passing with a light, easily re-framed base.',
    'Whole-body integration: this is one of the most fundamental modern guard passes specifically because it combines pressure and a small target area, making it hard to frame against even for an experienced partner.',
    'Common mistake: passing with the base too light or too upright, which gives the bottom person\'s frames and hip escapes room to reset guard before the knee actually clears.',
    'Coaching insight: the knee slice is often the first "pressure pass" taught precisely because it rewards heavy, connected weight distribution over speed — a slow, heavy knee slice usually beats a fast, light one.',
  ]},
  'Torreando (Bullfighter) Pass': {icon:'torreandoPass', video:'bjj torreando bullfighter pass technique tutorial', points:[
    'Setup: control both of the opponent\'s ankles or lower legs from a standing or kneeling position in front of them.',
    'Execution: step their legs to one side while moving your own hips to the opposite side — like a matador redirecting a bull — then move decisively to drop your weight onto a hip or side-control position the instant their legs clear.',
    'Whole-body integration: named for the bullfighting-style redirection at its core, this is typically taught as the first guard pass most beginners learn, because the underlying mechanic — redirect the legs, attack the space that opens — is so clear.',
    'Common mistake: redirecting the legs but then pausing before committing to the position, which gives the bottom person time to recover guard before the pass is actually finished.',
    'Coaching insight: a slow torreando gives real recovery time; this pass rewards decisiveness in the last step far more than raw grip strength on the ankles.',
  ]},
  'Stack Pass': {icon:'stackPass', video:'bjj stack pass technique tutorial', points:[
    'Setup: from inside the opponent\'s guard, gather their legs and hips and drive your weight forward and down, folding their hips up toward their own head.',
    'Execution: keep driving forward pressure through the shoulders while circling around the stacked legs to clear them to one side, rather than trying to yank a leg free with the arms alone.',
    'Whole-body integration: stacking uses gravity and body weight as the primary force, which is why it\'s a common pass for someone with a significant weight or pressure advantage over a partner with a flexible, active guard.',
    'Common mistake: stacking without maintaining forward pressure through the pass, which lets the bottom person\'s hips settle back down before the legs are actually cleared.',
    'Coaching insight: very flexible guard players are often harder to stack effectively, since they can absorb the compression without losing their frames — pairing the stack with a hip-clearing step is what actually finishes it.',
  ]},
  'Combat Base & Posture': {icon:'combatBase', video:'bjj combat base passing posture tutorial', points:[
    'Setup: from a kneeling or crouched passing position, keep one knee up and one knee down, hips low and weight centered directly over your base.',
    'Execution: practice being pushed and pulled off balance by a partner while resetting back into this same base immediately, rather than standing tall or squaring your hips.',
    'Whole-body integration: a stable combat base is what every guard pass in the sport is actually built on top of — without it, even a technically correct passing sequence falls apart under a strong, active guard.',
    'Common mistake: squaring the hips flat to the opponent, which offers no post to recover to if they off-balance you, versus a staggered base that always has a knee ready to plant.',
    'Coaching insight: this base is worth drilling under resistance specifically, since it only really matters once someone is actively trying to break it — a base that only works when nobody\'s pushing on it isn\'t solving the actual problem.',
  ]},
  'Mount Control & Maintenance': {icon:'mountControl', video:'bjj mount control maintenance tutorial', points:[
    'Setup: from top mount, sit with your weight centered on the opponent\'s torso, knees tucked in tight rather than floating high and light.',
    'Execution: as they bridge or try to shift you, ride the movement by posting a hand or foot and resettling your weight, instead of getting launched off by a single strong bridge.',
    'Whole-body integration: mount is treated in this drill as its own position to hold and maintain under resistance — before submissions are ever added on top, being genuinely hard to unseat from mount is the actual skill.',
    'Common mistake: posting the hands too far forward on the mat for balance, which is exactly the position a well-timed bridge and roll (Upa) is designed to exploit.',
    'Coaching insight: low, heavy knees generally beat a high, light seat — the higher the base, the easier it is for the bottom person\'s hips to create the gap a bridge needs to work.',
  ]},
  'Side Control Maintenance': {icon:'sideControl', video:'bjj side control maintenance tutorial', points:[
    'Setup: chest-to-chest and perpendicular across the opponent\'s torso, weight distributed heavily through the chest rather than propped up on the arms.',
    'Execution: as they try to shrimp, frame, or bridge to escape, follow their hip movement with your own hips to stay heavy and centered, rather than staying rigid in one spot.',
    'Whole-body integration: side control is often considered the most stable top pin in the sport precisely because it removes both the guard and the legs as tools for the bottom person, unlike mount or back control.',
    'Common mistake: basing up on the hands to "watch" for escapes, which lightens the pin considerably and gives the bottom person\'s frames something to push against.',
    'Coaching insight: a heavy, mobile side control — one that tracks the hips rather than staying frozen — is usually harder to escape than a stronger but static one.',
  ]},
  'Back Control & Hooks': {icon:'backControl', video:'bjj back control hooks technique tutorial', points:[
    'Setup: attached to the opponent\'s back with both hooks (feet/insteps) inside their thighs, and a seatbelt grip — one arm over the shoulder, one under the armpit.',
    'Execution: stay glued to their back through their movement, adjusting the hooks and seatbelt grip together rather than separately, so a shift in one always brings the other with it.',
    'Whole-body integration: back control is widely considered the single most dominant position in the sport, since it offers strong submission access while leaving the opponent almost no offensive options of their own.',
    'Common mistake: crossing the feet behind the opponent\'s back instead of hooking inside the thighs — crossed feet are vulnerable to a specific, well-known ankle-lock counter that hooks avoid entirely.',
    'Coaching insight: the seatbelt grip, not the hooks, is usually what\'s lost first when back control breaks down — re-securing the arm position tends to matter more moment-to-moment than the legs.',
  ]},
  'Knee-on-Belly': {icon:'kneeOnBelly', video:'bjj knee on belly technique tutorial', points:[
    'Setup: from side control, plant one knee directly on the opponent\'s stomach or lower chest, with the other leg extended out for base and mobility.',
    'Execution: keep weight actively sinking down through the planted knee rather than resting lightly, using the free leg to hop and adjust as they try to turn or bridge away.',
    'Whole-body integration: this position trades some stability for much better submission and transition access than a flatter pin like side control, since the free leg makes repositioning fast.',
    'Common mistake: staying flat-footed and static instead of using the free leg\'s mobility, which is the entire point of the position over a heavier, more static pin.',
    'Coaching insight: knee-on-belly is often as much a psychological pressure position as a physical one — the discomfort of a knee driven into the midsection frequently provokes a panicked, easily-exploited reaction from the bottom person.',
  ]},
  'Rear Naked Choke': {icon:'rearNakedChoke', video:'bjj rear naked choke drill technique', points:[
    'Setup: from back control with hooks or a body triangle in, thread one arm under the chin and the other hand grips behind the opponent\'s head or your own bicep, forming a figure-four.',
    'Execution: squeeze by pulling the choking arm\'s elbow in tight and expanding your chest, rather than trying to crank the neck side to side.',
    'Whole-body integration: back control itself — not the choke — is usually the harder part; drilling the choke\'s mechanics repeatedly from an already-secured back position builds the finish so it\'s automatic once the position is won.',
    'Common mistake: trying to finish before the choking arm is actually deep enough under the chin, which lets the opponent tuck their chin and defend the strangle entirely.',
    'Coaching insight: this submission is won mostly by the chest expanding and the elbow closing, not the arms curling — treating it as a "hug" rather than a "pull" tends to fix a stalled finish.',
  ]},
  'Armbar from Guard': {icon:'armbarGuard', video:'bjj armbar from guard drill technique', points:[
    'Setup: from closed guard, control one of the opponent\'s wrists and swivel your hips to bring a leg over their head, trapping the arm tightly between your thighs.',
    'Execution: keep the captured arm\'s thumb pointed up throughout the finish and extend your hips upward while pulling the wrist down, rather than yanking on the arm with your hands alone.',
    'Whole-body integration: this drill isolates the hip movement and arm control that make the armbar work — most failed armbars in live rolling come from skipping straight to the finish without first controlling the arm and hips correctly.',
    'Common mistake: letting the elbow drift away from the centerline during the hip swivel, which gives the opponent room to stack or pull the arm back out before the finish is secured.',
    'Coaching insight: the hip extension, not the hands, generates the finishing pressure — a strong hip bridge against a controlled, straight arm works even against much larger training partners.',
  ]},
  'Triangle Choke': {icon:'triangleChoke', video:'bjj triangle choke drill technique', points:[
    'Setup: from guard, control one of the opponent\'s arms across your body while bringing one leg up and over their same-side shoulder.',
    'Execution: lock the triangle by hooking that leg behind your own opposite knee, then adjust your angle so their trapped shoulder and your thigh compress both sides of their neck together.',
    'Whole-body integration: the choke is finished by pulling the opponent\'s head down and extending your hips, not by squeezing the knees together — the angle you finish at matters more than raw leg strength.',
    'Common mistake: locking the triangle at a flat angle directly in front of the opponent, rather than rotating to a steeper angle across their shoulder, which is usually why an otherwise correctly-locked triangle doesn\'t actually finish.',
    'Coaching insight: most triangle finishing power comes from correcting the angle after the lock, not from squeezing harder — this is why grip strength has surprisingly little to do with a well-executed triangle.',
  ]},
  'Kimura': {icon:'kimura', video:'bjj kimura shoulder lock drill technique', points:[
    'Setup: control one of the opponent\'s wrists and bend their elbow to roughly 90 degrees, then reach your other arm under theirs to grip your own wrist in a figure-four.',
    'Execution: rotate the trapped arm behind their back by lifting the wrist and rotating the shoulder, using the figure-four grip as a lever rather than pulling with the arms alone.',
    'Whole-body integration: the kimura is trained from multiple positions (guard, side control, back), since the underlying shoulder-rotation mechanic is identical regardless of which position it\'s entered from.',
    'Common mistake: trying to crank the shoulder before the figure-four grip and elbow angle are both properly secured, which telegraphs the submission early and gives the opponent time to defend the grip.',
    'Coaching insight: because the kimura is also a powerful control and transition tool even when it doesn\'t finish, it\'s often taught as much for the positions it opens up as for the submission itself.',
  ]},
  'Double Leg Takedown': {icon:'doubleLegTakedown', video:'bjj double leg takedown technique tutorial', points:[
    'Setup: level change by bending the knees (not the waist) to drop your hips below the opponent\'s, closing distance behind that level change.',
    'Execution: drive through both of their legs with your shoulder and wrap your arms around them, then drive forward with the legs to complete the takedown rather than trying to lift with the back.',
    'Whole-body integration: the level change and forward drive are a single connected motion in a good double leg — a takedown attempted from an upright stance loses most of the leverage the level change is meant to create.',
    'Common mistake: bending forward at the waist to reach for the legs instead of dropping the hips, which puts the head down and out of position and removes most of the driving power from the legs.',
    'Coaching insight: keeping the head up and to the side (not buried down) during the entry is what lets the takedown convert cleanly into a top position afterward, rather than ending in a scramble.',
  ]},
  'Closed Guard Pull': {icon:'closedGuardPull', video:'bjj guard pull technique tutorial', points:[
    'Setup: from a standing grip exchange, sit back toward the ground while pulling the opponent\'s sleeve or collar toward you.',
    'Execution: wrap your legs around their hips as you sit back, arriving in closed guard rather than flat on your back with no control.',
    'Whole-body integration: guard pulling is a deliberate strategic choice for grapplers who prefer to work from the bottom, and is common in gi and no-gi grappling where takedowns aren\'t the primary path to points or position.',
    'Common mistake: sitting straight down without a grip or without bringing the legs up to wrap, which just gives up top position for nothing in return.',
    'Coaching insight: a good guard pull secures a grip and closes the legs in the same motion the sit-back happens — treating it as one connected action, not sitting down first and figuring out the legs after.',
  ]},
  'Sprawl (Takedown Defense)': {icon:'sprawl', video:'bjj sprawl takedown defense technique tutorial', points:[
    'Setup: as an opponent shoots in for a takedown, react immediately by driving the hips back and down rather than backing away or standing tall.',
    'Execution: thrust the legs back explosively, dropping the hips and chest weight down onto the opponent\'s shoulders and upper back, while keeping the head up and posture forward rather than collapsing downward.',
    'Whole-body integration: a good sprawl is a whole-body, plyometric-style movement — hips driving back, legs extending explosively, and upper body weight dropping down all happen together, not as separate sequential steps.',
    'Common mistake: sprawling with the head down and chest collapsed forward, which loses the defensive positioning the sprawl is meant to create.',
    'Coaching insight: because a takedown attempt often arrives with very little warning mid-exchange, the sprawl needs to become a reflex through repetition — thinking through the steps in real time is generally too slow.',
  ]},
};

