    'Whole-body integration: because the catch is higher, a power snatch generally has to be lighter than a full snatch for the same lifter, but it builds the pulling speed and overhead stability at a higher, more athletic position.',
    'Common mistake: quarter-squatting under a weight that actually needed a deeper catch, which shows up as the bar being caught but immediately lost forward or backward from an unstable high position.',
    'Coaching insight: power variations are frequently used by coaches specifically to test and expose turnover speed and overhead stability weaknesses that a full-depth catch can otherwise "hide" by giving a lifter more time and range to recover into position.',
