    'Whole-body integration: the hip-hinge origin of this pose is the same fundamental hip-hinge pattern from Strength Fundamentals\' deadlift group — the body doesn\'t distinguish between "yoga hip hinge" and "strength training hip hinge," it\'s the same joint action either way.',
    'Common mistake: reaching for a hand position on the floor that\'s beyond current hamstring and hip flexibility, which typically rounds the lower back to gain the extra reach — a block or the shin is a completely legitimate substitute for the floor.',
    'Coaching insight: using a yoga block under the bottom hand is one of the most common and most effective modifications in this pose specifically because it lets the hip-hinge alignment stay correct regardless of current flexibility, rather than sacrificing alignment to chase a deeper reach.',
