    'Execution: lean back slightly while lifting the chest, engaging the core to keep the spine long rather than rounded, then progress toward lifting the feet and eventually straightening the legs only as core control genuinely allows.',
    'Whole-body integration: this pose directly mirrors the Hundred in Pilates — both are testing whether the deep core can support the spine against the combined load of the upper body and legs working against gravity at once.',
    'Common mistake: rounding through the lower back to achieve a "fuller" looking pose (straighter legs, hands off the ground) before the core actually has the strength to support that position — the rounded spine here is the same warning sign as in the Pilates Hundred.',
    'Coaching insight: keeping the hands lightly on the ground or behind the thighs is a legitimate, commonly used modification, not a sign of an incomplete pose — a long spine with support is a better training stimulus than a fully "advanced-looking" version with a rounded back.',
