const CYCLING_TECHNIQUE_DETAILS = {
  'Saddle Height & Position': {points:[
    'Setup: a reasonable starting point uses the LeMond formula (inseam measurement × 0.883, measured from the center of the bottom bracket to the top of the saddle) or the simpler heel method — heel on the pedal, pedaling backward, leg fully straight at the bottom of the stroke.',
    'Execution: once positioned, check that the knee shows a slight bend (not fully locked) when the ball of the foot is on the pedal at the bottom of the stroke — a commonly cited target range is roughly 25-35 degrees of knee bend at that point.',
    'Whole-body integration: saddle height affects nearly everything else in this card — too high forces the hips to rock side to side to reach the pedals (which shows up as inefficient pedaling and knee strain), too low reduces power output and increases knee stress from the opposite direction.',
    'Common mistake: setting saddle height once and never revisiting it as flexibility, cleat setup, or shoe changes shift the actual optimal position over time.',
    'Coaching insight: professional dynamic bike fitting (using motion capture or video analysis while actually pedaling) is considered more accurate than any static formula, but the LeMond formula and heel method both give a genuinely useful starting baseline that gets most riders very close without needing a professional fit session.',
  ]},
  'Handlebar Reach & Drop': {points:[
    'Setup: seated normally on the bike, hands resting on the brake hoods, checking for a slight, comfortable bend in the elbows rather than either fully locked arms or an overly compressed position.',
    'Execution: adjust stem length and handlebar height so the back position feels stable and sustainable for the intended ride duration, not just comfortable for a brief moment while standing still.',
    'Whole-body integration: reach and drop directly affect how much weight sits on the hands versus the saddle — a reach that\'s too long or too low shifts excess weight onto the hands and wrists, which shows up as numbness or discomfort well before the legs are actually tired.',
    'Common mistake: chasing an aggressive, low position for aerodynamics before the flexibility and core strength to sustainably hold it are actually in place — this often produces a worse, more fatigued position than a slightly less aggressive but genuinely sustainable one.',
    'Coaching insight: reach and drop are generally considered secondary adjustments to saddle height and position — most fitters address saddle setup first, since it affects power output and knee health, then fine-tune the front end for comfort and aerodynamics on top of a correct base position.',
  ]},
  'Cleat Positioning (Clipless Pedals)': {points:[
    'Setup: with the shoe off the bike, locate the ball of the foot (the widest part, at the base of the big toe) as the primary landmark for cleat placement.',
    'Execution: position the cleat so the ball of the foot sits directly over the pedal axle, with a neutral rotational angle that matches the foot\'s natural, relaxed stance rather than being forced into a fixed straight-ahead position.',
    'Whole-body integration: cleat position affects the whole kinetic chain up through the knee and hip — a cleat that\'s rotated to force an unnatural foot angle is a well-documented source of knee pain that has nothing to do with saddle height or pedaling technique.',
    'Common mistake: setting cleat rotation to a rigid, symmetrical position without accounting for natural foot angle, which forces the knee to compensate for a foot position that doesn\'t match how it actually wants to sit.',
    'Coaching insight: many bike fitters specifically check for natural, relaxed foot angle while off the bike (simply watching how the foot rests when walking or standing) before setting cleat rotation, rather than assuming a standard straight-ahead position works for everyone.',
  ]},
  'Core & Upper Body Stability on the Bike': {points:[
    'Setup: while riding, the core should be gently engaged throughout, similar to the bracing principle from Strength Fundamentals\' squat technique, adapted to a seated position.',
    'Execution: keep the upper body relatively still and stable while the legs do the work, avoiding excessive side-to-side rocking of the hips or shoulders, particularly noticeable when pedaling hard or climbing.',
    'Whole-body integration: a stable core and upper body means more of the leg\'s power actually translates into forward motion rather than being wasted on upper-body movement that doesn\'t contribute to propulsion.',
    'Common mistake: letting the upper body rock and sway to help generate pedaling power, which is both inefficient and a sign that core strength (trained off the bike in a general strength program) may be a limiting factor worth addressing directly.',
    'Coaching insight: this is a direct, practical reason cycling-focused athletes benefit from off-bike core training — the demand for a stable torso while the legs work hard is constant throughout a ride, and general core strength work translates directly to on-bike efficiency.',
  ]},
  'Pedal Stroke Efficiency (Circular vs. Mashing)': {points:[
    'Setup: think of the pedal stroke as a full circle, not just a downward push — the goal is applying smooth, continuous force through as much of that circle as possible.',
    'Execution: focus on pushing forward and down through the top of the stroke, driving through the bottom, and allowing the recovering leg to unweight (not actively pull up, which most riders don\'t need to consciously force) rather than only pushing hard on the downstroke and going dead through the rest of the circle.',
    'Whole-body integration: engaging the glutes, hamstrings, and calves throughout the stroke — not just the quads on the downstroke — distributes the workload across more muscle groups, which is directly relevant to fatigue management over a long ride.',
    'Common mistake: "mashing" — applying force almost exclusively on the downstroke while the rest of the pedal circle contributes very little — which is both less efficient and more fatiguing to the quads specifically than a fuller, more distributed stroke.',
    'Coaching insight: power meters and pedal-based training tools that show force distribution through the stroke have made this technique concept far more concrete for riders than it used to be — many riders significantly underestimate how "dead" the top and bottom of their own stroke actually is until they see it measured directly.',
  ]},
  'Cadence Selection': {points:[
    'Setup: cadence (pedal revolutions per minute) is a genuine training variable, not a fixed number to hold constant regardless of terrain or effort.',
    'Execution: generally favor a moderate-to-higher cadence (commonly in the 80-100 rpm range for road cycling, though this varies by rider and terrain) over a low-cadence, high-force approach, since higher cadence at a given power output generally shifts load away from the muscles and onto the cardiovascular system.',
    'Whole-body integration: cadence choice is a direct tradeoff between muscular fatigue and cardiovascular demand — a rider who mashes a low cadence will fatigue the legs faster, while a very high cadence at the same power shifts more of the burden onto breathing and heart rate.',
    'Common mistake: defaulting to whatever cadence feels natural without ever training cadence as its own skill, which leaves real efficiency gains unexplored, especially for riders who\'ve never deliberately practiced a higher-cadence, lower-force pedaling style.',
    'Coaching insight: dedicated cadence drills (deliberately riding at a higher cadence than feels natural for a set period) are a common training tool specifically because pedaling efficiency at higher cadence is a trainable skill, not just a fixed trait some riders have and others don\'t.',
  ]},
  'Standing Climb Technique': {points:[
    'Setup: shifting from seated to standing on a climb, hands typically moving to the brake hoods or the drops for better leverage.',
    'Execution: let the bike rock slightly side to side in rhythm with the pedal stroke while the body stays relatively centered over the bottom bracket, using bodyweight to help drive each downstroke rather than pedaling with the legs alone.',
    'Whole-body integration: standing climbing recruits more upper-body and core stability than seated climbing, trading some cardiovascular efficiency for a genuine power and leverage advantage on steeper sections.',
    'Common mistake: standing too early or too often on a long climb, which is more metabolically costly than seated climbing over time — standing is best used strategically (steeper pitches, attacks, or brief recovery from the saddle) rather than as a default climbing position.',
    'Coaching insight: many coaches specifically teach standing climbing as a tool to break up a long seated effort — alternating between seated and standing periodically can delay overall fatigue better than either position held continuously for the whole climb.',
  ]},
  'Seated Climb Technique': {points:[
    'Setup: seated on the saddle (not perched forward on the nose, which is a common but generally inefficient habit), hands on the hoods or tops depending on the gradient.',
    'Execution: maintain the same circular pedal stroke principles from the Pedaling Technique group, focusing on steady, sustainable power rather than surging, with the core braced to keep the upper body relatively still as effort increases.',
    'Whole-body integration: seated climbing is generally the more cardiovascularly efficient option for sustained climbs, which is why most successful long-climb pacing strategies favor staying seated except for short, deliberate standing efforts.',
    'Common mistake: sliding forward on the saddle to try to generate more power, which changes the effective saddle height and hip angle in a way that\'s rarely actually beneficial and is more often just an unconscious tension habit under effort.',
    'Coaching insight: the "steady, sustainable power" principle here connects directly to the Effort-Based Pacing technique later in this card — climbing pacing and overall ride pacing are the same underlying skill applied at different scales.',
  ]},
  'Cornering Technique & Line Choice': {points:[
    'Setup: approaching a corner, hold the handlebar in the drops for a more stable, lower center of gravity, and choose a line that uses the full width of the available road or path (wide entry, clipping the apex, wide exit) rather than a tight, direct line.',
    'Execution: drop the outside pedal to the bottom of the stroke and weight it firmly (the inside pedal stays up to avoid striking the ground), lower the torso along the top tube, and gently push the inside knee toward the top tube while the bike leans into the turn beneath a relatively upright body.',
    'Whole-body integration: this is the technique known as countersteering — a small, deliberate push on the inside of the handlebar initiates the bike leaning into the turn, letting the rider stay more balanced and centered while the bike itself does the leaning.',
    'Common mistake: sticking the inside knee outward into the turn (a habit borrowed from motorcycle road racing) rather than pressing it gently against the top tube — on a bicycle, this generally reduces stability rather than adding it.',
    'Coaching insight: cornering technique is widely recommended to be practiced deliberately in a low-risk setting (an empty parking lot with cones) before ever being relied on in real riding conditions — like any skill involving speed and balance, building confidence progressively matters more than attempting it correctly on the first try in traffic or a race.',
  ]},
  'Braking Technique': {points:[
    'Setup: use both brakes together for controlled speed reduction, with a generally heavier bias toward the front brake (which provides most of the stopping power) balanced against the rear brake for stability.',
    'Execution: complete the majority of braking before entering a corner, not during it — trail braking (light braking while already leaning into a turn) is an advanced technique that most riders should build up to gradually, not a default approach.',
    'Whole-body integration: braking technique and cornering technique are directly connected — braking hard mid-corner reduces the tire\'s available grip for cornering at the exact moment that grip is needed most, which is why "brake before, not during" is such a consistently repeated principle.',
    'Common mistake: grabbing the front brake hard and suddenly, especially mid-corner or on a loose surface, which can cause the front wheel to lose traction — smooth, progressive brake application is safer and more effective than an abrupt grab.',
    'Coaching insight: braking almost exclusively with the rear brake (out of an instinctive fear of going over the handlebars) is a common beginner habit that actually reduces overall stopping power, since the front wheel carries more of the bike\'s weight and traction under braking than most new riders intuitively expect.',
  ]},
  'Descending Technique & Body Position': {points:[
    'Setup: hands in the drops for stability and better brake leverage, weight shifted slightly back and low, pedals level (not turning) through technical sections.',
    'Execution: look ahead through the turn to where the bike is going (not down at the road immediately in front), applying the same cornering and braking principles from earlier in this card at higher speed and with less margin for error.',
    'Whole-body integration: descending is essentially cornering and braking technique performed at the point where mistakes are most consequential — it doesn\'t require fundamentally different skills, just the same skills executed with more precision and less room for error.',
    'Common mistake: tensing up and gripping the handlebars tightly as speed increases, which reduces the bike\'s ability to move naturally underneath a relaxed rider and often makes handling feel worse, not better.',
    'Coaching insight: experienced descenders consistently describe staying relaxed (loose grip, relaxed arms and shoulders) as one of the biggest differentiators in descending confidence and control — tension transmits directly into the bike\'s handling in a way that actively works against stability.',
  ]},
  'Group Riding & Drafting Position': {points:[
    'Setup: riding close behind another rider (drafting) to reduce wind resistance, typically positioning the front wheel a consistent, safe distance behind the wheel ahead.',
    'Execution: hold a steady line and consistent effort rather than surging or braking abruptly, communicate hazards to riders behind (calling out obstacles, potholes, or stopping), and take smooth, predictable turns at the front if rotating through a paceline.',
    'Whole-body integration: drafting provides a genuine, measurable energy savings (particularly noticeable at higher speeds), which is part of why pacing strategy in group settings differs meaningfully from solo riding — energy management is shared across the group, not carried entirely alone.',
    'Common mistake: sudden, unpredictable movements (braking abruptly, swerving, surging) while riding in a tight group, which is one of the more common causes of group riding incidents — smoothness and predictability matter more in a group than any individual rider\'s raw skill.',
    'Coaching insight: new group riders are frequently advised to spend real time specifically practicing steady, predictable riding at the back of a group before rotating to the front, since holding a consistent wheel position under fatigue is a genuinely different skill than simply being fit enough to keep pace.',
  ]},
  'Power/Effort-Based Pacing (Zones)': {points:[
    'Setup: whether using a power meter, heart rate monitor, or perceived effort, establish a rough sense of personal effort zones before a ride — an easy/endurance zone, a moderate/tempo zone, and a hard/threshold-or-above zone are a reasonable simple starting framework.',
    'Execution: use the appropriate zone for the intended purpose of a given ride or ride segment, rather than defaulting to whatever pace feels achievable in the moment — an easy ride held at a hard effort defeats its own purpose just as much as a hard interval held too easy does.',
    'Whole-body integration: this mirrors the exact same effort-based pacing principle from Running Form Fundamentals — the underlying training logic (train the intended zone, not just whatever pace happens to feel right today) applies across endurance sports generally, not just cycling specifically.',
    'Common mistake: riding most training rides at a moderate, "medium-hard" effort that\'s too hard to provide real easy-day recovery but too easy to provide a genuine hard-training stimulus — sometimes called the "gray zone," a well-documented pattern that undertrains both ends of the effort spectrum.',
    'Coaching insight: polarized training models (a large majority of easy-zone riding balanced against a smaller amount of genuinely hard effort, with less time in the ambiguous middle) are widely supported in endurance training research generally, cycling included, over a training plan dominated by moderate-effort riding.',
  ]},
  'Interval Pacing Strategy': {points:[
    'Setup: before starting a structured interval session, know the target effort, duration, and recovery period for each interval rather than deciding pacing reactively once already underway.',
    'Execution: aim for genuinely consistent effort across repeated intervals (not a fast first interval and progressively weaker later ones), adjusting the planned target down slightly if needed to hold that consistency rather than blowing up the structure of the whole session on an overambitious first effort.',
    'Whole-body integration: this connects directly to the same pacing discipline covered in Hyrox Fundamentals\' wall-ball and sled pacing sections — consistent, planned effort across repeated hard efforts is a transferable skill across very different sports.',
    'Common mistake: going out too hard on the first interval of a session because it feels achievable when fresh, then being unable to hold the same output on later intervals — a smoother, more sustainable set of intervals is generally a better training stimulus than one strong effort followed by a fade.',
    'Coaching insight: many structured training plans specifically prescribe interval targets slightly conservative relative to a rider\'s single best effort, precisely to protect the consistency of the whole set — the value of interval training comes from the accumulated, repeated stimulus, not from any single interval being maximal.',
  ]},
  'On-Bike Nutrition & Hydration Timing': {points:[
    'Setup: for rides under about an hour, water alone is generally sufficient; for longer or harder efforts, plan carbohydrate and fluid intake before starting, not reactively once already feeling depleted.',
    'Execution: begin taking in fluids and carbohydrates early in a longer ride, at regular intervals, rather than waiting until thirst or hunger is already noticeable — by the time those signals are strong, performance has typically already started to decline.',
    'Whole-body integration: this mirrors general endurance-sport fueling principles found across running and Hyrox pacing content in this app — waiting for hunger or thirst cues is a consistently identified mistake across endurance sports generally, not a cycling-specific issue.',
    'Common mistake: under-fueling on long rides specifically because eating or drinking feels like an interruption to effort or rhythm, when a brief pause to fuel properly protects the ability to maintain effort for the rest of the ride.',
    'Coaching insight: many experienced endurance coaches recommend practicing on-bike fueling strategy in training before ever relying on it in an event — both the amount and timing of intake, and how a rider\'s gut tolerates it under effort, benefit from being tested well before it actually matters.',
  ]},
  'Aerodynamic Position on Flats': {points:[
    'Setup: on flat terrain at speed, aerodynamic drag becomes the dominant resistance a rider is working against — a lower, narrower position genuinely reduces that resistance, but only if it can be held without excessive strain.',
    'Execution: lower the torso and narrow the position (hands in the drops or an aero bar position if available) while keeping breathing and pedaling technique from the earlier groups in this card intact — an aerodynamic position that compromises breathing or pedal stroke often costs more than it saves.',
    'Whole-body integration: this directly connects back to the flexibility and core strength prerequisites mentioned in the Handlebar Reach technique — a genuinely low, sustainable aero position depends on having built the flexibility and core endurance to hold it without it becoming its own source of fatigue.',
    'Common mistake: adopting an aggressive aero position without the flexibility or core strength to sustain it, which often results in a position that\'s abandoned well before the point in a ride where its aerodynamic benefit would have mattered most.',
    'Coaching insight: at typical road-cycling speeds, aerodynamic drag is widely cited as the dominant source of resistance a rider faces on flat terrain (far more than rolling resistance or drivetrain friction) — which is exactly why position, not just fitness, is such a heavily emphasized variable in performance-focused road and time trial cycling specifically.',
  ]},
};

